WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.009 --> 00:00:24.077
Everybody is an individual and
everybody is making decisions

00:00:24.077 --> 00:00:30.350
to benefit themselves either as
a consumer or as a business person.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.080
You want either more consumer goods or

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.120
more consumer satisfaction or
you want higher profits for your company.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.300
So the discipline of economics
is very individual oriented.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.710
We talk a little bit about
external benefits and

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.750
by the way that is really the rational for
subsidizing child care.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.650
That the benefits to the children
will benefit us all eventually.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.650
It's not just sticking with those
children and that's the rational for

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.430
subsidizing education.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.900
So from an employers
perspective child care matters,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.130
because your employees have
a place to send their child and

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.400
they're not worried all during the day
about what's happening to that child.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:21.930
And so peace of mind for employees
is very important to productivity.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.090
So it's a clear win-win.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:30.140
All of the research on Head Start
shows you the value of

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.390
taking kids at a young age and
making them ready for

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.010
kindergarten, and giving them
a loving supportive environment.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.390
So we're going to save a lot
of money in law enforcement,

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.020
we're going to save money
in remedial education.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.051
It's just so
clear that it's an important investment.

00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:57.720
[MUSIC]

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.678
When I first came to the business school,
this is 1972,

00:02:02.678 --> 00:02:06.860
Nixon had vetoed a childcare
bill in 1971 which

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.880
would have provided a national
system of childcare.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.400
And his rationale,
was that it would weaken the family.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.170
So I knew this, when I came here, but
I had doing research on the economics

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.130
of childcare and my very first
faculty seminar at the business school

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:32.540
was on the economics of childcare,
in which I argued or tried to argue,

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:38.510
that subsidizing childcare would be
a worthwhile economic investment.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.860
And my colleagues, all white men,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.160
conservative white men,
wouldn't let me give the seminar.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.270
They were so outraged at the notion
that I was proposing a subsidy for

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:56.430
child care, that they just talked among
each other about how outrageous this was.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.100
And I was too new to tell them
to be quiet so I could speak.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:06.000
And the person who was running
the seminar didn't tell them to be quiet.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.211
So I realized early on that there was
just tremendous opposition to this idea.

00:03:13.211 --> 00:03:17.948
[MUSIC]

00:03:17.948 --> 00:03:22.454
It would be a good investment for
the government to subsidize childcare for

00:03:22.454 --> 00:03:26.386
families who can't afford it and
for the rest of the population,

00:03:26.386 --> 00:03:30.020
it would be good to ensure
good quality childcare.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.550
A lot of the childcare that
exists now is not good quality.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:39.490
And so all the research is done says that
childcare is beneficial to children.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:43.270
But that research is done
on good quality childcare.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:47.678
Because poor quality childcare
doesn't let researchers in.

00:03:47.678 --> 00:03:51.973
[LAUGH] So we know that a lot of
the care is not good quality and

00:03:51.973 --> 00:03:56.532
we know that this is not a good
thing that our children are being

00:03:56.532 --> 00:04:02.220
raised in situations that are not
helping them to grow and develop.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.850
At a national level,
I would like to see a childcare system.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.290
We don't have a childcare system.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.390
We have lots of people
trying to get childcare and

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:16.960
one of the women, who comes to talk
in my class, is a GSB graduate.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.640
She's married to another GSB graduate,
their issue is not financial.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:29.310
They have enough money, but when she
became pregnant, she put her child

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:35.370
on a waiting list at a childcare center
the day she knew that she was pregnant.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:40.140
The child was born approximately
nine months later and

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.040
she wanted to go back to work
three months after that.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.140
So it was almost a year that the child
had been on the waiting list.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.910
And they had no slot for the child.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.750
It didn't matter what she was
willing to pay, there was no slot.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.670
The other question about
childcare is affordability.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:04:59.730
That was not her issue,

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:05.530
but many people cannot afford
the childcare that does exist.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:10.537
We need a child care system and
we need it to be subsidized for

00:05:10.537 --> 00:05:13.049
people who need the subsidy.

00:05:13.049 --> 00:05:19.116
[MUSIC]

00:05:22.366 --> 00:05:27.001
Fremont was proposing a ballot initiative

00:05:27.001 --> 00:05:31.764
that would cost homeowners, $1 a month,

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:37.300
$12 a year to provide
after school childcare for

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.950
Fremont Public School children.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:45.530
And 86% of the population voted this down.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.920
And my student was doing an exit poll.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.198
Of course,
we didn't know how the vote would come,

00:05:52.198 --> 00:05:56.758
but we were interested in why people
were voting however they had voted.

00:05:56.758 --> 00:06:01.228
And what he found was that
most people said it's

00:06:01.228 --> 00:06:05.850
not my problem if people
don't have childcare.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.402
They should figure it out.

00:06:07.402 --> 00:06:11.820
I don't have to pay $12 a year
to help them figure this out.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.360
This is not my problem.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.370
I think we need to look globally.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:21.950
We need to look beyond just
our own satisfaction or

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:23.610
our country's satisfaction.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.730
Because we are a global economy.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.520
We are a global entity.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.670
And I'll never forget when

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:39.800
the astronauts saw the Earth from the
moon, and saw how we were a single entity.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:43.550
And pollution in one place
affects pollution in another.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.330
And nuclear power affects the whole world.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:54.350
So I think all of us need to be
thinking more broadly than ourselves or

00:06:54.350 --> 00:07:00.400
our families to our country and
then to the world as a whole.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.618
And I think that's the challenge
of our current moment,

00:07:05.618 --> 00:07:09.107
is how to get us to think more broadly.

00:07:09.107 --> 00:07:13.389
[MUSIC]

